WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.760
Welcome to the Explainer. Look, if you're a technical leader, a CTO, VP of engineering, or frankly, anyone steering the technical ship right now, you already know that just slapping an API key onto a legacy product is not a long-term strategy.

2
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:20.420
We are right in the middle of a fundamental shift in how we build systems. Today, we're diving into the new paradigm known as software 2.0.

3
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:27.200
Because AI, it isn't just a shiny new feature to tack onto your backlog. It's a fundamentally non-deterministic infrastructure.

4
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.620
And successfully scaling it means you've got to throw out the old rulebook

5
00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.300
and adopt an entirely new set of engineering playbooks.

6
00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.000
So here's our roadmap for today.

7
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:42.400
We'll start with the software 2.0 paradigm, then tackle the big question, build, fine-tune, or API.

8
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.820
After that, we'll look at inference costs and latency, data leakage, rag versus hallucinations,

9
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.440
and finally, how to survive model drift.

10
00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.500
All right, let's jump right into section one, the software 2.0 paradigm.

11
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.960
time. To really grasp software 2.0, you have to think about how we used to build things.

12
00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.800
In software 1.0, a programmer sat down and wrote explicit rigid rules. If the email contains the

13
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:11.200
word Viagra, mark it as spam, right? You manually defined every single edge case. But here is the

14
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:16.460
crucial shift. In software 2.0, we stop writing those rules. Instead, we feed neural networks

15
00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.720
massive amounts of data and basically let the system independently discover the statistical

16
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:25.060
patterns on its own. You show the network 10 million marked emails, and it figures out what

17
00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:29.200
spam looks like without you holding its hand. The model isn't actually thinking like a human,

18
00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:33.540
it's predicting based on deep statistical patterns. And really understanding that difference,

19
00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.080
that is absolutely key to unlocking new product opportunities that were literally impossible

20
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:45.220
just five years ago. Moving on to section two, the foundational choice, build, fine-tune, or API.

21
00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:51.140
So there's a pretty harsh reality to AI production economics, and you absolutely have to separate

22
00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.760
the training phase from the inference phase.

23
00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.280
Pre-training a massive model from scratch

24
00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.480
requires reading essentially the entire internet.

25
00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.780
We're talking massive, one-time capital expenditures here.

26
00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.220
Millions of dollars, thousands of GPUs,

27
00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.040
months of nonstop computation.

28
00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:07.980
Inference, on the other hand,

29
00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:10.620
is what happens when your user actually asks a question.

30
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.520
That is a continuous, transactional, per-query cost.

31
00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.640
It's measured in seconds and cents.

32
00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:19.120
Every single token generated costs you money.

33
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:23.720
As a technical leader, you have got to structure your unit economics around this continuous

34
00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:24.960
operational expense.

35
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:27.760
Which brings us to your core infrastructure options.

36
00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.780
Should you use an API, fine-tune an existing model, or just build one from scratch?

37
00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:36.460
Well, using an off-the-shelf API is incredibly fast, it's cheap, and it's perfect for initial

38
00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:37.200
proofs of concept.

39
00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.060
But, you know, it means vendor lock-in and a lot less control.

40
00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.020
Fine-tuning an existing model, that gives you vastly better performance for your specific

41
00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:47.640
domain, but it requires clean proprietary data and specialized training costs.

42
00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:52.180
And finally, building a model from scratch gives you total control and a unique competitive

43
00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:56.560
advantage, but it requires massive capital and a dedicated world-class AI research team.

44
00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:58.660
So how on earth do you choose?

45
00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.700
Well, there's a golden rule of thumb here.

46
00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:04.860
Don't build what you can buy and don't buy what you can use for free.

47
00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:10.600
You should always start with an API to prove that the AI actually delivers real value to

48
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:11.140
your users.

49
00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:17.060
Only step up to fine tuning or the astronomical cost of building from scratch if you have a

50
00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:22.020
strict, undeniable business necessity, and the unique proprietary data to back it up.

51
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:28.380
All right, section three, inference costs and latency. In traditional software, we obsessed

52
00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:35.080
over page load times, right? Well, in software 2.0, we obsess over TTFT, time to first token.

53
00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:39.920
Research shows that users will tolerate a wait of only two to three seconds for an AI response

54
00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:44.800
before they just abandon the interaction. Because AI generation takes time, you have to architect

55
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.280
your UX to handle this latency. You can't just leave users hanging, staring at a blank screen.

56
00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.840
You need to implement streaming interfaces, loading states, and intermediate content to

57
00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:58.000
bridge that two to three second gap. It's literally a make or break product constraint.

58
00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:02.740
And honestly, if you're working in computer vision or audio, it gets even more intense.

59
00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:07.860
Let's say you're analyzing real-time video, maybe for an autonomous vehicle or live sports tracking.

60
00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.880
Your edge inference constraints are brutal. You need less than 30 milliseconds of latency

61
00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:18.680
per frame just to keep up. This usually forces you to completely abandon heavy cloud APIs and

62
00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:24.080
figure out how to run highly optimized smaller models directly on edge devices. Which brings us

63
00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:30.160
to section four, data leakage and privacy. Let's talk about data leakage. It is the silent killer

64
00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:34.820
of machine learning projects. Data leakage happens when you use a feature during the model's training

65
00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:39.520
phase that simply will not be available when the model is running live in production. You are

66
00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:44.840
basically, inadvertently, giving the model the answer key during its test. The result? Your

67
00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:48.700
model looks like an absolute genius in the lab, but the second you deploy it to production,

68
00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:54.100
it completely falls apart. Let me give you a very real, classic example of this trap.

69
00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:59.260
Imagine your team is building a model to predict customer churn. They feed the historical data

70
00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:04.720
in and wow, the model is 99% accurate. High fives all around, right? But wait,

71
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:09.740
They accidentally included the database column for cancellation date as a predictive feature.

72
00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.120
Well, of course the model knows who is going to churn.

73
00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:14.120
It's literally looking at the date they canceled.

74
00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:20.100
But in the real world, when you're trying to predict if an active customer might cancel next month, that field is going to be blank.

75
00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:28.370
To avoid disastrous mistakes like that, your data science team has to perform rigorous exploratory data analysis, or EDA.

76
00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:32.290
Think of this as the site survey before you pour the foundation for a house.

77
00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:36.510
Before writing a single line of model code, you must look for red flags.

78
00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:40.890
Do you have extreme imbalance, where 99.9% of your examples are negative?

79
00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:44.330
Are you missing values in over 50% of your critical features?

80
00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:48.250
Is there inherent bias, where the data only represents a single demographic?

81
00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:54.230
If you don't catch these things in the EDA phase, any model you build on top of it will be fundamentally compromised.

82
00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:58.770
And this right here is why deep domain knowledge is non-negotiable.

83
00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:00.950
Consider this actual real-world case.

84
00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:03.810
A team built an AI to detect pneumonia from x-rays.

85
00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:06.530
It hit 95% accuracy in testing.

86
00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:07.510
Amazing, right?

87
00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:09.390
But when an actual doctor reviewed it,

88
00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:12.650
they realized the AI wasn't looking at the lungs at all.

89
00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:13.350
Nope.

90
00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:16.710
It had learned to recognize the specific font of the text label

91
00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:17.750
in the corner of the x-ray

92
00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:21.010
that belonged to a hospital known for a high rate of severe cases.

93
00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:23.770
Without domain experts in the loop during data analysis,

94
00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:26.270
your brilliant math might just be learning hospital fonts

95
00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:27.990
instead of actually detecting diseases.

96
00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:32.170
Let's move on to section five, rag versus hallucinations.

97
00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:36.110
When you're dealing with language models, you have a clear escalation path for injecting

98
00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:36.450
knowledge.

99
00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:39.350
You should always start with prompting for general tasks.

100
00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:41.010
It's cheap, it's fast, it works.

101
00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:45.270
But when your model needs specific, factual, and up-to-date knowledge about your proprietary

102
00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:48.450
business, you step up to rag, which we'll define in a second.

103
00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:53.370
You should reserve expensive fine-tuning only for when you need the model to adopt a

104
00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:57.550
highly specific tone or behavior, like speaking exactly in your brand's unique voice.

105
00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.510
Please, don't fine-tune just to teach the model facts.

106
00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:03.310
It is way too expensive and an absolute nightmare to update.

107
00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:05.380
So, what is RAG?

108
00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:08.100
It stands for Retrieval Augmented Generation,

109
00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:11.520
and it is exactly how you beat AI hallucinations.

110
00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:13.380
Step 1. Retrieval

111
00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:16.020
A user asks about your company's return policy,

112
00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:20.320
and the system searches your secure database for the actual return policy document.

113
00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:22.000
Step 2. Augmented

114
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:27.580
The system grabs that document and secretly pastes it into the prompt alongside the user's question.

115
00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:33.300
Step 3. Generation. The model now reads your actual document and generates an answer strictly

116
00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:38.100
based on that text. By doing this, you're securely grounding the AI in your reality,

117
00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:41.360
completely preventing it from just making up a policy that doesn't exist.

118
00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:48.140
Finally, Section 6. Surviving Model Drift. You see, traditional software mostly stays

119
00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:53.480
exactly the same once it's deployed. AI models? They absolutely do not. They suffer from model

120
00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:58.240
drift. This is the inevitable degradation of an AI model's predictive power in production

121
00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:03.980
simply because real-world data and user behaviors constantly change over time. Think about it. If

122
00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:09.560
you train a model on data from 2022 and user habits shift drastically by 2024, your model's

123
00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:14.480
accuracy is going to plummet. Deploying a model without rigorous continuous monitoring is literally

124
00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:19.400
planting a ticking time bomb in your infrastructure. And because of this drift, your team has to adopt

125
00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:24.880
cyclic, non-linear methodologies like the CRISP-DM cycle. You don't just go straight from step one to

126
00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:29.800
step six and then head to the golf course. It's a loop. You evaluate in step five, realize you need

127
00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:34.840
more features, and jump back to step three for data prep. Or you deploy in step six, model drift

128
00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:39.380
hits six months later, performance drops, and you loop right back to step four for remodeling.

129
00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:43.840
Architecting software 2.0 means building infrastructure that naturally supports

130
00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:49.840
these continuous, repeating maintenance loops. So to guarantee long-term reliability, you need

131
00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:54.600
a holistic strategy. You can't just measure technical accuracy in a vacuum. You must tie

132
00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:59.680
your model's performance to actual business KPIs, like reducing false positives without hurting

133
00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:05.700
overall revenue. For any high-stakes outputs, medical, financial, legal, you absolutely must

134
00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:10.520
enforce a human in the loop to catch the weird edge cases the AI misses. And of course, you must

135
00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:15.100
actively monitor for that model drift and have automated pipelines ready to go for continuous

136
00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:20.460
retraining cycles. As you look at the architecture of your next big AI project, you really have to

137
00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:25.680
ask yourself a tough question. Are you building a resilient, cyclical loop that monitors, adapts,

138
00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:30.340
and learns over time? Or are you just deploying a brittle endpoint that will confidently hallucinate

139
00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:35.060
its way to failure the moment the real world changes? Architecting Software 2.0 is fundamentally

140
00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:40.060
about planning for the unknown. Build smart, monitor constantly, and never ever trust a model

141
00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:43.220
blindly. Thanks so much for joining me on this explainer. See you next time.

